    text: "Why This System Works: Golf improvement is not random. It follows a predictable pattern: golfers who build strong fundamentals improve faster, retain their gains longer, and perform better under pressure than those who chase quick fixes or swing changes without a foundation.\nThe Dominus Golf Blueprint is built on that pattern. It is a step-by-step development system that starts where scoring actually happens — on the putting green — and builds outward through the short game, irons, and driver. Each phase reinforces the one before it. Skills compound. Progress accelerates.\nThis is not a collection of tips. It is a system with a clear sequence, a clear purpose, and clear standards for what good looks like at every stage.\nThe Five Foundational Principles\n1. Start with Control: Shorter, more manageable movements create the foundation for stronger and more reliable swings.\nWhen a golfer can control the basics, it becomes easier to add speed and power later. Control is not the enemy of distance — it is the prerequisite for it.\n2. Build Through Repetition: Practice is most effective when it reinforces the same movement patterns again and again. The goal is not to practice until you get it right — it is to practice until you cannot get it wrong. Repetition builds the motor pathways that make good swings automatic under pressure.\nThe 100 Rule: Every day, make at least 100 swings or putts. Tour Pure swings, club swings, balls hit, and putts all count. If a session comes in under 100, finish with Tour Pure rhythm swings (“TOUR… PURE”) or putts until you reach 100. Rest days count too: 100 slow Tour Pure swings or 100 carpet putts at home.\n3. Keep Movement Simple: The more unnecessary motion a golfer removes from the swing, the easier it becomes to repeat that swing consistently. Simple mechanics are reliable mechanics. Every drill in this guide is designed to eliminate complexity and build clarity.\n4. Use Feel to Guide Improvement: Golfers improve not only by thinking about technique, but by developing a clear physical sense of what correct motion feels like. That body awareness — knowing when something is right without having to think about it — is what separates good golfers from great ones.\n5. Let Practice Shape Performance: Visualization, alignment, and structured drills help connect what a golfer sees with what the body does. Over time, that connection builds the automatic responses that produce confident, repeatable play on the course.",
