    title: "Swing path, swing plane and body rotation",
    text: "Two concepts underpin everything in this guide: swing path and swing plane. Understanding them is not optional — they are the foundation of consistent ball striking.\nSwing Path: Swing path is the direction the clubhead is traveling at the moment of impact, relative to the target line. There are three path directions:\n• In-to-Out: The clubhead travels from inside the target line to outside it through impact. This promotes a draw.\n• Square: The clubhead travels directly along the target line through impact. This produces a straight shot.\n• Out-to-In: The clubhead travels from outside the target line to inside it through impact. This promotes a fade or, when excessive, a pull or slice.\nMost golfers who struggle with consistency have an out-to-in path — the most common cause of the slice. Fixing swing path is often the single highest-leverage change a golfer can make.\nSwing Plane: Swing plane is the angle of the swing relative to the ground. Think of it as the tilted circle the club travels on from address through the backswing, downswing, and follow-through. A swing that stays on plane produces consistent contact. A swing that goes too steep (above plane) or too flat (below plane) requires compensations that break down under pressure.\nThe ideal plane is influenced by your height, arm length, and the club you are using. Longer clubs produce a flatter plane naturally. Shorter clubs are more upright. The key is consistency — the club should return to impact on approximately the same plane it started on.\nHow Body Rotation Drives Everything: The most important thing to understand about both path and plane is that they are driven primarily by body rotation, not arm movement. Golfers who try to steer the clubhead with their arms produce inconsistent paths and planes. Golfers who rotate their body correctly through the shot — hips leading the downswing, chest clearing through impact, weight transferring to the front foot — find that the club follows a consistent, repeatable path almost automatically.\nEvery chapter in this guide will return to this principle: rotation drives the swing. Arms and hands deliver the club. The body is the engine.",
