    text: "Weight Distribution by Shot Type: Weight distribution at address and through the swing is one of the most overlooked fundamentals in golf instruction. Here is how it works across every shot type:\nPutting: 50/50 at address — stays 50/50 throughout. Putting is the only shot in golf where weight distribution remains completely equal and static. Any weight shift during the putting stroke introduces lower body movement, which changes the arc of the putter and the path through impact.\nChipping and Pitching: 60/40 favoring the lead foot at address and throughout the stroke. This forward lean promotes a descending strike and clean contact. The weight does not shift during a chip or pitch — the slight forward lean is set at address and maintained.\nWedge Shots (Partial Swings): 55/45 favoring the lead foot at address, shifting to approximately 70/30 through impact. The body rotates but the weight transfer is controlled and measured, matching the shorter swing length.\nIrons (Full Swing): 50/50 at address, shifting to 70/30 or greater favoring the lead foot at impact. The downswing is initiated by the lower body — the hips rotate and the weight shifts forward before the arms and club arrive at the ball.\nDriver: 55/45 favoring the trail foot at address. Because the ball is teed up and positioned forward in the stance, the driver is the only club where you want to feel slightly behind the ball at impact to produce an ascending strike. Weight transfers fully to the lead foot through follow-through.",
